1
00:00:15,521 --> 00:00:22,165
[SPEAKER_06]: So, who can tell me what is samādhi?

2
00:00:29,229 --> 00:00:33,952
[SPEAKER_03]: Establishment of the mind.

3
00:00:34,853 --> 00:00:35,173
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

4
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:36,474
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you describe that?

5
00:00:37,974 --> 00:00:39,335
[SPEAKER_06]: What is establishment of the mind?

6
00:00:46,415 --> 00:00:50,039
[SPEAKER_03]: Seeing the sign of the mind is one way to describe it.

7
00:00:52,222 --> 00:01:03,935
[SPEAKER_03]: So, eh... any description will be a thought or an image.

8
00:01:05,396 --> 00:01:07,559
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it's always... the mind always proceeds.

9
00:01:08,306 --> 00:01:19,150
[SPEAKER_06]: Any description that you can give... Yeah, but still... Somebody comes to the Buddha and wants to learn what is samādhi.

10
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:20,311
[SPEAKER_06]: You can just stick to samādhi.

11
00:01:20,431 --> 00:01:23,332
[SPEAKER_06]: You don't have to describe the mind and the sign and what's behind.

12
00:01:23,932 --> 00:01:25,332
[SPEAKER_06]: Just, what is samādhi?

13
00:01:25,352 --> 00:01:27,793
[SPEAKER_06]: I mean, we translate it as composure.

14
00:01:28,574 --> 00:01:34,476
[SPEAKER_06]: So, can you describe what is composure to somebody that doesn't really know what to look for?

15
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:40,415
[SPEAKER_06]: So, give me just practical, even mundane examples of what is composure.

16
00:01:41,136 --> 00:01:50,586
[SPEAKER_03]: Instead of getting drawn into the content of thoughts or images, one sees those

17
00:01:51,584 --> 00:01:53,245
[SPEAKER_03]: Thoughts or images as thoughts.

18
00:01:55,527 --> 00:01:58,949
[SPEAKER_03]: So, one becomes established on the nature of one's experience.

19
00:01:59,329 --> 00:01:59,649
[SPEAKER_06]: Okay.

20
00:02:00,530 --> 00:02:07,514
[SPEAKER_06]: But before we get talking about that level, can anybody give me a mundane example of composure?

21
00:02:08,175 --> 00:02:15,620
[SPEAKER_06]: Because people do experience, say somebody who doesn't practice the dhamma, would experience throughout their life forms of composure.

22
00:02:16,861 --> 00:02:18,422
[SPEAKER_06]: So, what is that composure?

23
00:02:18,842 --> 00:02:20,423
[SPEAKER_06]: Just give me some examples of it.

24
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:39,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Not being bothered or moved or disturbed by disturbing events, let's say, or particular circumstances that arise, you cannot be shaken by them.

25
00:02:39,875 --> 00:02:44,439
[SPEAKER_06]: So, like, for example, if somebody is in a good mood, for whatever reason,

26
00:02:48,790 --> 00:02:55,054
[SPEAKER_06]: They go throughout the day in a good mood and even bad things or bad people don't necessarily affect them.

27
00:02:56,915 --> 00:03:01,798
[SPEAKER_06]: So, it's not... the reason why I'm coming on to this is because it's not just the composure of an idea.

28
00:03:02,699 --> 00:03:04,840
[SPEAKER_06]: It also... well, it is partially.

29
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:05,100
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

30
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:09,523
[SPEAKER_06]: But that idea implies emotional composure as a background of it.

31
00:03:11,777 --> 00:03:14,638
[SPEAKER_06]: So, yeah, mood would be an example.

32
00:03:15,018 --> 00:03:23,041
[SPEAKER_06]: Equally, someone's mind can be composed upon a not-so-good mood, which means even good things would annoy them and irritate them.

33
00:03:23,201 --> 00:03:23,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

34
00:03:23,521 --> 00:03:31,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're kind of... anything that happens comes within that sort of bad mood.

35
00:03:32,405 --> 00:03:41,248
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything is... has a kind of a disagreeable baseline, I suppose, or an agreeable baseline, if they're in a good mood.

36
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:44,542
[SPEAKER_06]: So, composure then.

37
00:03:46,084 --> 00:03:50,087
[SPEAKER_06]: Does a person have a say in their mood?

38
00:03:52,249 --> 00:03:52,449
[SPEAKER_06]: No?

39
00:03:52,649 --> 00:03:52,809
[SPEAKER_06]: No.

40
00:03:53,790 --> 00:03:53,950
[SPEAKER_06]: No.

41
00:03:54,250 --> 00:03:56,292
[SPEAKER_06]: Really?

42
00:03:56,312 --> 00:03:57,173
[SPEAKER_01]: I would say yes.

43
00:03:57,813 --> 00:03:58,094
[SPEAKER_06]: Really?

44
00:04:00,636 --> 00:04:02,617
[SPEAKER_06]: So, if you say no, why is it no?

45
00:04:02,757 --> 00:04:06,260
[SPEAKER_00]: I think there's no personal... you can't change it.

46
00:04:06,561 --> 00:04:09,063
[SPEAKER_00]: It presents it of its own.

47
00:04:09,952 --> 00:04:11,213
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, that's true on that level.

48
00:04:11,514 --> 00:04:12,795
[SPEAKER_06]: And if you say yes, why is that?

49
00:04:14,596 --> 00:04:36,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is possible, like even in mundane terms, like my father would say, no, I can put myself in a good mood by thinking about certain things, reminding myself of how

50
00:04:36,834 --> 00:04:50,149
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose fortunate I am compared to other people, so, you know, I can keep my mood not from going too bad or too good by doing certain things and by reflecting in certain ways.

51
00:04:50,209 --> 00:04:51,511
[SPEAKER_06]: And actually, yeah, both are right.

52
00:04:52,232 --> 00:04:57,157
[SPEAKER_06]: So, what you were referring to is the aspect of, yeah, mind is on its own.

53
00:04:58,478 --> 00:05:01,680
[SPEAKER_06]: Moods of the mind are phenomenon on its own.

54
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:08,884
[SPEAKER_06]: However, your actions, as in, are you allowing the unrestrained wild animal mind to go where it wants?

55
00:05:09,384 --> 00:05:10,285
[SPEAKER_06]: Moods will get worse.

56
00:05:11,245 --> 00:05:12,286
[SPEAKER_06]: Moods will get depressed.

57
00:05:12,766 --> 00:05:16,268
[SPEAKER_06]: Moods will get, like, agitated, or along those lines.

58
00:05:16,628 --> 00:05:18,169
[SPEAKER_06]: Or are you making the effort to restrain it?

59
00:05:18,489 --> 00:05:19,930
[SPEAKER_06]: Moods will get lighter and better.

60
00:05:20,831 --> 00:05:27,775
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you don't have a say in the phenomenon of the mood, but you do actually have a say whether you're indirectly feeding it or not.

61
00:05:28,985 --> 00:05:30,366
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's that recognition.

62
00:05:30,386 --> 00:05:45,961
[SPEAKER_06]: And exactly like, you know, as the Buddha said, the one who doesn't live a celibate life, a householder in the suttas, if he frequently recollects the Buddha, the dhamma, the saṅgha, the virtue, he will get pleasant abiding of a type of samādhi here and now.

63
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:52,062
[SPEAKER_06]: So, the mind will be composed upon this frequent reflection upon certain values.

64
00:05:52,902 --> 00:06:01,125
[SPEAKER_06]: Even when you don't feel like thinking about those things, you make the effort to think about those things and make a practice out of reflecting upon those things day in, day out.

65
00:06:01,765 --> 00:06:08,588
[SPEAKER_06]: And those effectual composures of the mind, as the Buddha said, are pleasant abiding for the level of somebody who is not a celibate.

66
00:06:09,417 --> 00:06:19,426
[SPEAKER_06]: Now, of course, if you want to have higher pleasant abidings, you need to restrain more, but the point is, it comes down to that frequent recollection and reflection.

67
00:06:19,726 --> 00:06:21,928
[SPEAKER_06]: That is what will incline the mind.

68
00:06:22,289 --> 00:06:27,113
[SPEAKER_06]: And what I said, what one frequently thinks and ponders on, that's where the mind is going to go.

69
00:06:27,854 --> 00:06:29,996
[SPEAKER_06]: So, is it sensuality or non-sensuality?

70
00:06:30,036 --> 00:06:34,700
[SPEAKER_06]: Is it irritation, fault-finding, ill-will or non-irritation, not fault-finding, not ill-will?

71
00:06:36,007 --> 00:06:40,150
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's that background practice, that reflection.

72
00:06:41,211 --> 00:06:51,497
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how is then composure, frequent recollection, different from concentration?

73
00:06:55,162 --> 00:06:58,985
[SPEAKER_03]: So, concentration, do you mean in terms of, like, focusing practices?

74
00:06:59,165 --> 00:07:00,145
[SPEAKER_06]: I mean concentration.

75
00:07:00,406 --> 00:07:03,948
[SPEAKER_06]: Okay, let's say, what is concentration, in the most mundane terms?

76
00:07:04,248 --> 00:07:05,749
[SPEAKER_06]: How would you describe concentration?

77
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:08,451
[SPEAKER_06]: Somebody says, hey, what's concentration?

78
00:07:08,471 --> 00:07:18,298
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yeah, it is a kind of... you're... at the expense of everything else, you're honing in on something particular.

79
00:07:19,249 --> 00:07:19,469
[SPEAKER_06]: Aha.

80
00:07:19,969 --> 00:07:27,914
[SPEAKER_06]: So, if I don't have any experience in anything, and I come and ask you, I heard this word concentration.

81
00:07:28,894 --> 00:07:29,675
[SPEAKER_06]: How do I do that?

82
00:07:30,295 --> 00:07:31,256
[SPEAKER_05]: What is concentration?

83
00:07:32,536 --> 00:07:34,778
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how would you just advise me, in most mundane terms?

84
00:07:35,438 --> 00:07:38,460
[SPEAKER_06]: Just pick an object and focus on it.

85
00:07:40,227 --> 00:07:49,896
[SPEAKER_01]: It could be as mundane as like... Yeah, just mundane examples of concentration.

86
00:07:50,277 --> 00:07:59,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to play the piano, being very focused on having something that is totally absorbing all of your attention.

87
00:08:00,225 --> 00:08:08,890
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sense of, I have to focus on this thing, and if I forget it for a second, I'm going to make a mistake in my piano playing, so I can't do that.

88
00:08:08,910 --> 00:08:13,793
[SPEAKER_06]: So, it comes down to attention, to detail, to a particular thing that's in front of you.

89
00:08:14,794 --> 00:08:24,120
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how is that, or is that, attention to a particular object of your senses, or even idea, for that matter?

90
00:08:25,338 --> 00:08:29,531
[SPEAKER_06]: Is that different from recollection that we just talked about?

91
00:08:29,711 --> 00:08:30,353
[SPEAKER_06]: The composure?

92
00:08:31,296 --> 00:08:31,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

93
00:08:31,978 --> 00:08:32,259
[SPEAKER_06]: How?

94
00:08:33,344 --> 00:08:35,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it depends upon that object.

95
00:08:36,286 --> 00:08:36,587
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

96
00:08:36,767 --> 00:08:37,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, what?

97
00:08:37,227 --> 00:08:41,350
[SPEAKER_01]: It depends on that object that your mind is focused on.

98
00:08:41,791 --> 00:08:43,552
[SPEAKER_06]: What depends on the object?

99
00:08:43,592 --> 00:08:44,033
[SPEAKER_06]: Concentration.

100
00:08:44,053 --> 00:08:44,533
[SPEAKER_06]: Concentration.

101
00:08:44,553 --> 00:08:45,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Concentration.

102
00:08:45,534 --> 00:08:45,954
[SPEAKER_06]: How?

103
00:08:46,415 --> 00:08:47,335
[SPEAKER_06]: Where is the dependence?

104
00:08:47,696 --> 00:08:57,263
[SPEAKER_06]: Because you could say, I don't care what I'm concentrated on, I practice concentration of everything, anything that comes into my field of attention, I attend to it.

105
00:08:57,643 --> 00:08:58,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, okay, I see what you mean.

106
00:09:03,570 --> 00:09:08,515
[SPEAKER_01]: It depends on that object basically staying the same and not going away, because if you're sort of...

107
00:09:08,535 --> 00:09:19,205
[SPEAKER_01]: I concentrate on anything that comes into my field of attention, then you wouldn't call that really concentration, because it would be diverse, like it would be any type of object that...

108
00:09:19,705 --> 00:09:21,086
[SPEAKER_06]: But that's my point.

109
00:09:21,226 --> 00:09:24,349
[SPEAKER_06]: Does it matter what object it is in order to define a concentration?

110
00:09:24,409 --> 00:09:25,390
[SPEAKER_06]: So, it's what you do.

111
00:09:25,530 --> 00:09:25,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

112
00:09:25,950 --> 00:09:27,111
[SPEAKER_01]: It's how you...

113
00:09:27,352 --> 00:09:34,097
[SPEAKER_06]: So, then you could concentrate on any different object that come your way and still practice concentration, even if they're different objects.

114
00:09:34,117 --> 00:09:34,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

115
00:09:35,799 --> 00:09:36,559
[SPEAKER_06]: So, then...

116
00:09:37,580 --> 00:09:38,461
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how's that different?

117
00:09:38,801 --> 00:09:39,502
[SPEAKER_06]: Or is that different?

118
00:09:39,782 --> 00:09:40,563
[SPEAKER_06]: Or why is that different?

119
00:09:40,603 --> 00:09:46,387
[SPEAKER_06]: Because, like, if we say recollecting the certain themes,

120
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:51,880
[SPEAKER_06]: Can you concentrate on them?

121
00:09:52,040 --> 00:09:52,801
[SPEAKER_06]: Is that what we are saying?

122
00:09:53,061 --> 00:09:53,761
[SPEAKER_06]: Can that be there?

123
00:09:54,841 --> 00:09:57,482
[SPEAKER_06]: So, what's the difference then, if you're concentrating?

124
00:10:00,163 --> 00:10:05,365
[SPEAKER_05]: That one is concentrating, one is attending a thing, whereas recollection...

125
00:10:07,392 --> 00:10:13,674
[SPEAKER_05]: As I understand, it's more about discerning, so to say, or seeing the background, but seeing...

126
00:10:13,754 --> 00:10:22,456
[SPEAKER_06]: In like mundane terms, you could say one of the most obvious differences is when you're concentrating, you are doing it.

127
00:10:22,556 --> 00:10:24,116
[SPEAKER_06]: You are attending what's there.

128
00:10:25,317 --> 00:10:29,918
[SPEAKER_06]: But when you're recollecting, there is already a certain degree

129
00:10:31,356 --> 00:10:32,136
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, it's indirect.

130
00:10:32,157 --> 00:10:33,277
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

131
00:10:33,317 --> 00:10:35,738
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you set up, as Buddha said, you know, like...

132
00:10:55,008 --> 00:10:57,670
[SPEAKER_06]: The water comes down the mountain and it starts filling the pools.

133
00:10:57,690 --> 00:11:00,392
[SPEAKER_06]: And so, imagine now, you're just... you can't stop that water.

134
00:11:00,992 --> 00:11:01,473
[SPEAKER_06]: Or you can't...

135
00:11:01,693 --> 00:11:06,076
[SPEAKER_06]: I mean, you can try, but the point is to direct it elsewhere.

136
00:11:06,156 --> 00:11:09,578
[SPEAKER_06]: By setting this context, they will then encourage you to go in that direction.

137
00:11:10,319 --> 00:11:11,119
[SPEAKER_06]: Oh, yeah, don't look there.

138
00:11:11,139 --> 00:11:11,559
[SPEAKER_06]: Look here.

139
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:12,120
[SPEAKER_06]: Look at this.

140
00:11:12,180 --> 00:11:13,201
[SPEAKER_06]: And it's bad over there.

141
00:11:13,241 --> 00:11:21,967
[SPEAKER_06]: And all this danger, peril, lack of satisfaction, and so on, are the themes that, if you sustain, the mind will turn away from it.

142
00:11:24,173 --> 00:11:26,294
[SPEAKER_06]: And why can't you do that with concentration?

143
00:11:32,058 --> 00:11:34,779
[SPEAKER_06]: Why can't you just concentrate on your mind and make it go that way?

144
00:11:35,420 --> 00:11:38,582
[SPEAKER_05]: Because it's not an object that you can attend.

145
00:11:38,822 --> 00:11:39,102
[SPEAKER_06]: Exactly.

146
00:11:39,122 --> 00:11:42,624
[SPEAKER_06]: People keep thinking that concentration is the mind watching.

147
00:11:42,704 --> 00:11:45,305
[SPEAKER_06]: You don't see your mind when you're concentrated with it.

148
00:11:46,546 --> 00:11:48,587
[SPEAKER_06]: It's not a sense object.

149
00:11:48,888 --> 00:11:50,088
[SPEAKER_06]: It's always before.

150
00:11:50,128 --> 00:11:50,669
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, exactly.

151
00:11:50,729 --> 00:11:52,390
[SPEAKER_06]: So, in the same sense, I will

152
00:11:53,307 --> 00:11:54,507
[SPEAKER_06]: It's actually exactly the same.

153
00:11:54,667 --> 00:12:02,310
[SPEAKER_06]: If I were to be looking at visual things to see my eyes there, that is impossible.

154
00:12:03,691 --> 00:12:08,732
[SPEAKER_06]: Or feeling tactile sensations to see my body there, that is impossible.

155
00:12:09,152 --> 00:12:14,414
[SPEAKER_06]: Body is the background, body is the organ for your sensations.

156
00:12:15,895 --> 00:12:20,236
[SPEAKER_06]: So then, can concentration be useful?

157
00:12:21,397 --> 00:12:22,197
[SPEAKER_05]: In terms of practice?

158
00:12:27,049 --> 00:12:36,857
[SPEAKER_03]: Initially, I mean, it's a prerequisite for reading the suttas, getting information, getting data, in terms of... Well, attending to right things, sure.

159
00:12:37,838 --> 00:12:43,923
[SPEAKER_06]: But I'm talking about practice of concentration in itself.

160
00:12:45,464 --> 00:12:46,545
[SPEAKER_06]: Can that be beneficial?

161
00:12:58,282 --> 00:13:11,446
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, if the mind is inclining towards unwholesome things, it could be helpful to intentionally direct it towards more wholesome things.

162
00:13:12,366 --> 00:13:15,007
[SPEAKER_04]: But can you discern wholesome when you're concentrated?

163
00:13:15,747 --> 00:13:16,687
[SPEAKER_04]: Is that a possibility?

164
00:13:17,847 --> 00:13:20,128
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, some things are obviously unwholesome.

165
00:13:20,928 --> 00:13:27,050
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, yes, but while being concentrated, do you have the possibility of discerning whether you are attending to something wholesome or not?

166
00:13:34,056 --> 00:13:35,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by virtue of the concentration.

167
00:13:35,877 --> 00:13:38,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Or if you could, but it would be at the expense of the concentration.

168
00:13:39,099 --> 00:13:39,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

169
00:13:40,279 --> 00:13:45,422
[SPEAKER_06]: So then, the question comes down to recognizing that degree or clarifying it.

170
00:13:45,922 --> 00:13:49,464
[SPEAKER_06]: Where does attention become concentration practice?

171
00:13:50,945 --> 00:13:53,926
[SPEAKER_06]: Like, you cannot but not attend anything.

172
00:13:55,207 --> 00:14:01,210
[SPEAKER_06]: But you can certainly learn how not to overlook the background, the context.

173
00:14:02,615 --> 00:14:06,717
[SPEAKER_06]: The meaning, the intentionality behind whatever you're attending currently.

174
00:14:07,697 --> 00:14:10,799
[SPEAKER_06]: And that context is what tells you.

175
00:14:10,819 --> 00:14:12,259
[SPEAKER_06]: Because that's where your intentions are.

176
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:29,607
[SPEAKER_06]: That's what tells you whether this is wholesome, and I should allow it to be attended further, or unwholesome, and I should not welcome it, not delight it, and make sure I don't attend, as the Buddha said, particular signs and features that are inducive of lust and would increase the unwholesome state.

177
00:14:32,050 --> 00:14:41,995
[SPEAKER_06]: But it doesn't say, I'll just focus on it harder, concentrate, because, see, even if even if it's a good thing,

178
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:49,823
[SPEAKER_06]: Say, if it's a wholesome thing, if you actually think, okay, if I double down now in attending to it, I'll get there faster.

179
00:14:49,983 --> 00:14:51,784
[SPEAKER_06]: You want to distract yourself further.

180
00:14:52,264 --> 00:14:53,385
[SPEAKER_06]: It always has to be done.

181
00:14:54,025 --> 00:14:59,127
[SPEAKER_06]: It always has to be done at the expense of the context, at the expense of composure.

182
00:14:59,287 --> 00:15:00,588
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, that's what I meant.

183
00:15:01,648 --> 00:15:07,031
[SPEAKER_06]: So, ironically, the concentration practice is directly opposite of samādhi practice.

184
00:15:09,448 --> 00:15:18,492
[SPEAKER_06]: Samādhi being the composure of the theme, of the context, recollecting things rightly, that then sustain and incline your mind in certain way.

185
00:15:19,132 --> 00:15:25,175
[SPEAKER_06]: Concentration, basically, means overshooting the mark and abandoning the context.

186
00:15:25,335 --> 00:15:28,877
[SPEAKER_06]: Even if it's, as I said, concentrating upon the good things.

187
00:15:32,921 --> 00:15:39,443
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's why it's that the whole practice is to learn how not to overshoot or, like, undershoot, in a sense of ignore the whole thing, of course.

188
00:15:40,204 --> 00:15:54,549
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, attend to something, but don't let it become a focusing concentration object in practice, because you cannot possibly do that and at the same time sustain the background context of your intentionality, the meaning, the mood behind all of that.

189
00:16:09,017 --> 00:16:25,710
[SPEAKER_06]: So then, can anybody elaborate or give some examples on, as we said, composure equals establishment of the mind, equals right recollections, right themes, right context, equals putting the work there.

