00:47:09,515 --> 00:47:25,447
[SPEAKER_06]: You know, you get the intellectual understanding, and then you start certain recollections, reflections, evaluations, dwelling on values, on benefits, on drawbacks, just day in, day out, just... and you might approach it through different themes, but you're doing the same recollection practice.

528
00:47:25,507 --> 00:47:27,749
[SPEAKER_06]: It's on the basis of your virtue, sense, restraint and solitude.

529
00:47:28,529 --> 00:47:30,050
[SPEAKER_06]: Your mind will then become visible.

530
00:47:30,711 --> 00:47:34,474
[SPEAKER_06]: You will start seeing this background leaning towards this or towards that.

531
00:47:35,134 --> 00:47:37,336
[SPEAKER_06]: And those are the signs and features of the mind.

532
00:47:38,257 --> 00:47:39,798
[SPEAKER_06]: That's how you know the mind is there.

533
00:47:39,998 --> 00:47:40,739
[SPEAKER_06]: Oh, footprints.

534
00:47:42,620 --> 00:47:43,842
[SPEAKER_06]: He must have passed through here.

535
00:47:45,503 --> 00:47:46,504
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's how you find it.

536
00:47:47,765 --> 00:47:54,731
[SPEAKER_06]: So, the samādhi described here now, you could see how it's just part of the same development.

537
00:47:57,390 --> 00:48:21,549
[SPEAKER_06]: So, because many people would recognize the value, because you can't deny, that's all the suttas talk about, the value of, you know, recollecting, re-evaluating, thinking, right themes, and frequently pondering upon, but then they would still think that the place for concentration is sort of... there is a place for concentration as a separate practice to this, that helps one another.

538
00:48:22,209 --> 00:48:24,211
[SPEAKER_06]: It doesn't.

539
00:48:24,351 --> 00:48:26,553
[SPEAKER_06]: You can only concentrate at the expense of a context.

540
00:48:28,643 --> 00:48:30,444
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you want to practice samādhi.

541
00:48:31,064 --> 00:48:41,166
[SPEAKER_06]: Samādhi, as the suttas say, is just composing and steadying down and fortifying your recollectedness, your mindfulness.

542
00:48:42,307 --> 00:48:57,391
[SPEAKER_06]: So, your themes, your recollections, your frequently thinking and pondering, do that now, even more organized manner, like go under a root of a tree in a closed room and think and ponder even longer,

543
00:48:58,598 --> 00:49:01,700
[SPEAKER_06]: Even more persistently, and that is your samādhi practice.

544
00:49:03,121 --> 00:49:15,731
[SPEAKER_06]: So, it wouldn't be surprising then, that the result of that would be the first jhāna, which is defined by thinking and pondering, free from greed, aversion, delusion, free from hindrances.

545
00:49:17,614 --> 00:49:22,596
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how do you free your thinking and pondering from the parasites of greed, aversion, delusion?

546
00:49:22,816 --> 00:49:26,297
[SPEAKER_06]: Well, by thinking and pondering rightly, they will take it away.

547
00:49:26,317 --> 00:49:28,558
[SPEAKER_06]: They will purify it like you purify the gold.

548
00:49:28,798 --> 00:49:30,639
[SPEAKER_05]: By directing the mind away from this.

549
00:49:30,779 --> 00:49:31,099
[SPEAKER_06]: Exactly.

550
00:49:31,139 --> 00:49:35,400
[SPEAKER_06]: By slowly taking it away and removing the impurities, in that sense.

551
00:49:36,260 --> 00:49:43,343
[SPEAKER_05]: But I think as long as one does not recognize, as you described earlier, that there is this peripheral

552
00:49:44,813 --> 00:49:50,915
[SPEAKER_06]: If there are these peripheral phenomena, so to say, then the latter would kind of make sense?

553
00:49:51,276 --> 00:49:53,456
[SPEAKER_06]: Well, yes, because... Because there is nothing else.

554
00:49:53,496 --> 00:49:58,158
[SPEAKER_05]: So what can I do, except... rather than catch every moment, so to say?

555
00:49:58,478 --> 00:50:04,661
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, you can learn, intellectually first, that catching the moment is taking you the wrong way.

556
00:50:05,261 --> 00:50:06,401
[SPEAKER_06]: And recognize it for yourself.

557
00:50:06,602 --> 00:50:08,002
[SPEAKER_06]: So you start refraining from it.

558
00:50:08,142 --> 00:50:12,404
[SPEAKER_06]: And now that you know you're not supposed to be doing it, that's already a step forward.

559
00:50:14,196 --> 00:50:23,584
[SPEAKER_06]: But yes, everything you do, from the baseline of where your attention is, which is over-attention, let's say like that, everything you do will be overly attended.

560
00:50:24,224 --> 00:50:27,187
[SPEAKER_06]: Everything you do will be made an object of your attention.

561
00:50:27,667 --> 00:50:29,088
[SPEAKER_06]: That's why the mind is invisible.

562
00:50:32,571 --> 00:50:42,920
[SPEAKER_06]: But, if your views change, and you don't think you should be attending to things like that anymore, well, now you have a chance, actually, to discern the middle ground.

563
00:50:44,470 --> 00:51:06,357
[SPEAKER_06]: But if your views don't change, then you're just going to be sort of creating a patchwork of trying to include everything that you find helpful and so on, you know, while at the same time ignoring the bigger picture, which is the fact that, well, the suttas don't talk about it, certainly not in the way commonly approached, and composure is the result of the right mindfulness.

564
00:51:07,318 --> 00:51:08,499
[SPEAKER_06]: That's the result of the right view.

565
00:51:09,080 --> 00:51:17,227
[SPEAKER_06]: So, right view, right mindfulness, right intention, means right samādhi, right composure.

566
00:51:17,387 --> 00:51:19,469
[SPEAKER_03]: Because those are the things that you compose.

567
00:51:27,035 --> 00:51:30,996
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, but seven enlightenment factors apply to somebody who is at least Sotāpanna.

568
00:51:31,516 --> 00:51:33,577
[SPEAKER_06]: You need to have a right view for for any of them.

569
00:51:34,557 --> 00:51:42,359
[SPEAKER_00]: And how would you describe, within this context, the practice, the practical way of guarding the sense doors?

570
00:51:43,259 --> 00:51:43,499
[SPEAKER_06]: Same.

571
00:51:44,079 --> 00:51:47,240
[SPEAKER_06]: Not losing the sight of where the problem is.

572
00:51:47,720 --> 00:51:55,542
[SPEAKER_06]: So, practically speaking, right here, right now, do you guard your eyes now by focusing on

573
00:51:57,062 --> 00:52:00,003
[SPEAKER_06]: Lumps in your head and feeling sensations and abdomen.

574
00:52:00,803 --> 00:52:06,525
[SPEAKER_06]: Or do you guard your eyes, not by thinking about your eyes, but by thinking about the peril of things that you shouldn't look at?

575
00:52:09,366 --> 00:52:12,426
[SPEAKER_06]: Feeling that danger peripherally, not where your eyes are.

576
00:52:13,187 --> 00:52:18,428
[SPEAKER_06]: Because, see, if I focus on my eyes, I'll do so at the expense of the context of what I shouldn't be looking at.

577
00:52:19,308 --> 00:52:23,970
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you guard your sense doors by thinking rightly as to what you should

578
00:52:24,953 --> 00:52:27,034
[SPEAKER_06]: Allow your eyes to look and what you shouldn't.

579
00:52:27,755 --> 00:52:35,178
[SPEAKER_06]: And it is when you forget the danger of looking at the unwholesome things, that's when you intentionally choose to look.

580
00:52:35,999 --> 00:52:39,581
[SPEAKER_06]: That's when you allow those wild animals of your eyes to go and look.

581
00:52:40,401 --> 00:52:41,722
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you guard your eyes now.

582
00:52:42,699 --> 00:52:56,512
[SPEAKER_06]: By basically being mindful of pretty much your situation as a whole, that in case something unsuitable for your eyes comes from some direction, you don't look at it, and if you look at it, you don't grasp pleasing signs and features.

583
00:52:58,934 --> 00:53:06,621
[SPEAKER_06]: So, it's everywhere, but where the concentration of your eye is, or on your sights... sights, yeah, the objects of your eyes.

584
00:53:08,984 --> 00:53:11,906
[SPEAKER_06]: Because you can't know, I'll just look down, I'll just look at this carpet, I'll just look at this carpet.

585
00:53:12,507 --> 00:53:16,170
[SPEAKER_06]: Even that is defined by the actual context of why you're doing it.

586
00:53:16,951 --> 00:53:20,173
[SPEAKER_06]: But do you think that just looking down here will make the problems go away?

587
00:53:20,914 --> 00:53:21,054
[SPEAKER_06]: No.

588
00:53:21,955 --> 00:53:31,503
[SPEAKER_06]: The risk of looking unwholesome, the risk of being infected by unwholesome, infected by lust, aversion and delusion, that's not in what you see.

589
00:53:32,584 --> 00:53:37,528
[SPEAKER_06]: That's on the level of sense bases, the level of that background, the context, stuff you cannot directly attend to.

590
00:53:38,062 --> 00:53:39,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Also implied in the word guarding.

591
00:53:39,802 --> 00:53:41,523
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not an active changing.

592
00:53:41,543 --> 00:53:42,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

593
00:53:42,083 --> 00:53:44,643
[SPEAKER_06]: Guarding does not mean tackling down every single one.

594
00:53:44,663 --> 00:53:46,104
[SPEAKER_06]: No, it just means protect the doorway.

595
00:53:46,844 --> 00:53:51,265
[SPEAKER_06]: All you need to do is make sure the handle is locked and door is barred.

596
00:53:51,785 --> 00:53:52,705
[SPEAKER_06]: That's how you guard it.

597
00:53:53,345 --> 00:53:56,406
[SPEAKER_06]: Not opening the door and then punching everybody that tries to come in.

598
00:53:59,087 --> 00:53:59,267
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah.

599
00:54:01,107 --> 00:54:04,668
[SPEAKER_06]: So, and again, virtue, guarding of the sense doors,

600
00:54:05,980 --> 00:54:07,961
[SPEAKER_06]: leads to samādhi, if it's done rightly.

601
00:54:10,282 --> 00:54:22,029
[SPEAKER_06]: Because, what else is guarding of my senses, other than established context, that now defines my values, or where will I let my eyes, ears, and other senses go?

602
00:54:27,453 --> 00:54:33,734
[SPEAKER_06]: So, something seemingly so simple like, oh, recollecting the Buddha, the dhamma, the saṅgha, the virtue... Yeah, yeah, yeah, it's fine.

603
00:54:34,355 --> 00:54:35,775
[SPEAKER_06]: That is the base for samādhi.

604
00:54:36,475 --> 00:54:37,375
[SPEAKER_06]: For a lay person.

605
00:54:37,575 --> 00:54:50,038
[SPEAKER_06]: And then, if, you know, if your precepts are upgraded, up to eight precepts and so on, it can, on its own, result in jhāna and thinking and pondering that's free from greed, aversion, delusion, if you spend days, months, who knows how long.

606
00:54:51,104 --> 00:54:57,487
[SPEAKER_06]: Of cultivating these right moods that become imperturbable towards greed, aversion, and delusion, and distraction.

607
00:54:59,388 --> 00:55:09,853
[SPEAKER_06]: Then, all you need to do is just... because, again, you did the work, you have cultivated it, the animal has traveled, the mind has gone in the right direction for a long time.

608
00:55:10,753 --> 00:55:12,634
[SPEAKER_06]: Now, all you need to do is just stay there.

609
00:55:13,695 --> 00:55:20,038
[SPEAKER_06]: Just stay in that secluded environment, be moderate in eating, and this context will be fueled by those right things.

610
00:55:24,345 --> 00:55:27,406
[SPEAKER_06]: And same like with Brahmaviharas, the loving kind, same.

611
00:55:29,667 --> 00:55:34,669
[SPEAKER_06]: It's the establishment of the context of friendliness, non-ill-will.

612
00:55:37,490 --> 00:55:41,552
[SPEAKER_06]: But it's not a technique that will result in this experience of it.

613
00:55:41,572 --> 00:55:43,833
[SPEAKER_06]: No, it's something you have to frequently reflect upon.

614
00:55:44,793 --> 00:55:48,335
[SPEAKER_06]: For yourself, for others, in every direction, up and down, doesn't matter.

615
00:55:49,435 --> 00:55:53,697
[SPEAKER_06]: Until no part has been left

616
00:55:54,952 --> 00:55:57,713
[SPEAKER_06]: Unreflected, to put it that way.

617
00:55:59,034 --> 00:56:04,576
[SPEAKER_06]: And if you frequently think and ponder about it, the mind will incline towards friendliness, whether you want it or not.

618
00:56:05,096 --> 00:56:15,320
[SPEAKER_06]: And the mind has been inclining and traveling in the direction of friendliness long enough, even meeting unfriendly people will not perturb you, or certainly not as much as before.

619
00:56:16,740 --> 00:56:18,221
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, and that's completely different from

620
00:56:19,816 --> 00:56:24,939
[SPEAKER_05]: When someone is... someone I meet is disagreeable, or is... And then I do metta.

621
00:56:24,999 --> 00:56:25,459
[SPEAKER_06]: Exactly.

622
00:56:25,539 --> 00:56:25,739
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah.

623
00:56:25,859 --> 00:56:26,379
[SPEAKER_06]: Counter it.

624
00:56:26,479 --> 00:56:26,859
[SPEAKER_06]: Antidote.

625
00:56:26,919 --> 00:56:27,200
[SPEAKER_06]: Yes.

626
00:56:27,400 --> 00:56:28,220
[SPEAKER_06]: Hatred means metta.

627
00:56:28,300 --> 00:56:28,540
[SPEAKER_06]: Do it.

628
00:56:28,560 --> 00:56:28,720
[SPEAKER_06]: Do it.

629
00:56:28,981 --> 00:56:29,241
[SPEAKER_06]: Why?

630
00:56:29,801 --> 00:56:31,202
[SPEAKER_06]: Not because you're really friendly.

631
00:56:31,282 --> 00:56:34,783
[SPEAKER_06]: Just because you want to get rid of the discomfort of witnessing unfriendly persons.

632
00:56:35,244 --> 00:56:37,165
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you're averse to what you're feeling.

633
00:56:37,625 --> 00:56:38,485
[SPEAKER_06]: And back on to what I said.

634
00:56:38,505 --> 00:56:42,467
[SPEAKER_06]: Most people's practice comes down to being averse towards their own states of mind.

635
00:56:42,507 --> 00:56:43,428
[SPEAKER_06]: Trying to get rid of them.

636
00:56:43,928 --> 00:56:46,629
[SPEAKER_03]: So, all you can do, when it arises, is just endure it.

637
00:56:46,669 --> 00:56:47,750
[SPEAKER_03]: Because anything you do,

638
00:56:49,407 --> 00:56:57,412
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, all you can do is, yeah, like, endure it in a sense of don't act towards it.

639
00:56:57,512 --> 00:57:00,554
[SPEAKER_06]: But, see, now we just define what you can do.

640
00:57:00,594 --> 00:57:05,857
[SPEAKER_06]: Carry on evaluating, re-evaluating, thinking about Buddha, dhamma, saṅgha, virtue, friendliness.

641
00:57:05,877 --> 00:57:08,439
[SPEAKER_03]: You can, you can, but it won't last, though.

642
00:57:13,894 --> 00:57:16,796
[SPEAKER_06]: Because it's not directly dealing with what's bothering you.

643
00:57:16,836 --> 00:57:22,479
[SPEAKER_06]: See, it already implies recognition that what I'm trying to get rid of is not actually the problem.

644
00:57:23,079 --> 00:57:25,841
[SPEAKER_06]: The problem is the mind hasn't traveled far enough in the right direction.

645
00:57:26,521 --> 00:57:30,843
[SPEAKER_06]: So, it can be a motivator to reflect.

646
00:57:30,863 --> 00:57:38,848
[SPEAKER_06]: And even if you initially do it to get rid of it, again, the direction of getting rid of it is opposite of evaluating, re-evaluating, recollecting rightly.

647
00:57:39,428 --> 00:57:42,670
[SPEAKER_06]: So, in other words, you won't be able to do it for those reasons much longer.

648
00:57:43,570 --> 00:57:44,350
[SPEAKER_03]: If it's authentic.

649
00:57:45,490 --> 00:57:51,271
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, provided you already see that relationship between you and your own mind, which is what citta nimitta is.

650
00:57:51,992 --> 00:57:59,713
[SPEAKER_06]: If you see it as a thing that's there, that inclines the here or there, depending on what you keep providing and allowing it to feed on.

651
00:58:01,513 --> 00:58:05,474
[SPEAKER_00]: That should be the initial stage of trying to get rid of craving by craving.

652
00:58:05,954 --> 00:58:07,114
[SPEAKER_06]: Exactly, yeah.

653
00:58:07,554 --> 00:58:11,675
[SPEAKER_06]: So, yeah, there's craving involved, but see, now it's not actually fueling more craving.

654
00:58:12,757 --> 00:58:14,118
[SPEAKER_06]: You're not acting out of that aversion.

655
00:58:14,958 --> 00:58:16,159
[SPEAKER_06]: You're acting, okay, yeah, fine.

656
00:58:16,399 --> 00:58:17,300
[SPEAKER_06]: I want to get rid of this.

657
00:58:17,440 --> 00:58:18,041
[SPEAKER_06]: It's unpleasant.

658
00:58:18,061 --> 00:58:19,121
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah, I want to abandon it.

659
00:58:19,842 --> 00:58:22,403
[SPEAKER_06]: Do I abandon it by cultivating more ill-will towards it?

660
00:58:22,564 --> 00:58:22,684
[SPEAKER_06]: No.

661
00:58:22,884 --> 00:58:28,067
[SPEAKER_06]: I abandon it by making my context even less perturbable.

662
00:58:29,508 --> 00:58:32,670
[SPEAKER_06]: Up until it becomes imperturbable and even more imperturbable.

663
00:58:34,672 --> 00:58:36,052
[SPEAKER_06]: And there you go.

664
00:58:36,152 --> 00:58:36,793
[SPEAKER_06]: You abandon it.

665
00:58:38,370 --> 00:58:39,370
[SPEAKER_06]: The need to deal with it.

666
00:58:39,730 --> 00:58:40,111
[SPEAKER_06]: Exactly.

667
00:58:40,231 --> 00:58:40,691
[SPEAKER_06]: The pressure.

668
00:58:41,731 --> 00:58:42,671
[SPEAKER_06]: Being affected by it.

669
00:58:44,172 --> 00:58:45,732
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's how you go beyond the hindrances as well.

670
00:58:46,473 --> 00:58:49,894
[SPEAKER_06]: They're the symptoms of where your mind is.

671
00:58:49,914 --> 00:59:00,757
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you go beyond them, by enduring them, and by cultivating the context that cannot be perturbed by those hindrances, and then, they cease to be... well, they cease to hinder you, as I said so many times.

672
00:59:06,096 --> 00:59:10,498
[SPEAKER_06]: So, then, it's just agreeable, disagreeable, and everything else in between.

673
00:59:10,959 --> 00:59:18,803
[SPEAKER_06]: But none of that can overpower, overwhelm, and limit your mind.

674
00:59:20,864 --> 00:59:29,228
[SPEAKER_06]: So, that's often why samādhi, then, is referred to as boundless, unconstricted, nothing mystical about it.

675
00:59:30,709 --> 00:59:47,503
[SPEAKER_06]: If your wholesome mood, on account of right recollection in the Buddha, Dhamma, Saṅgha, has been cultivated for years actively, on account of your virtue and sense restraint, there's going to be some pretty unshakable samādhi, pretty unconstrained.

676
00:59:48,764 --> 00:59:52,887
[SPEAKER_06]: No worldly constraints can sort of limit it and bring it down, that mood.

677
00:59:54,412 --> 01:00:04,518
[SPEAKER_06]: On account of that mood, you will be factually joyful, happy, with a calm body and content, because it's a good mood.

678
01:00:06,919 --> 01:00:14,003
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, you cannot take offense anymore, because taking involves sort of leaping out into that thing which is offending you.

679
01:00:14,023 --> 01:00:15,624
[SPEAKER_04]: You're moving in the opposite direction.

680
01:00:15,984 --> 01:00:16,164
[SPEAKER_06]: Yeah.

681
01:00:16,944 --> 01:00:17,485
[SPEAKER_06]: In other words, the

682
01:00:18,432 --> 01:00:23,274
[SPEAKER_06]: The attempt to take it, if the Mahārāja presents it, is within the good mood.

683
01:00:23,314 --> 01:00:28,576
[SPEAKER_06]: In other words, good mood can handle even the attempt to take it without fearing it, without trying to get rid of it or prevent it.

684
01:00:29,956 --> 01:00:31,637
[SPEAKER_06]: It's included within it.

685
01:00:35,050 --> 01:00:47,180
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it occurred to me that, from a mundane point of view, somebody might be sitting in an office, having to write a very difficult email, and they might say to their colleagues, I want to concentrate on writing this difficult email.

686
01:00:47,840 --> 01:01:01,991
[SPEAKER_04]: But what they actually end up doing is closing the door to the office, so that they can be alone, and then they recollect the importance of sending this email properly, being aware of the consequences if they do it wrongly, but they might assume that this is

687
01:01:02,712 --> 01:01:12,724
[SPEAKER_04]: Concentration, called like that, but what they actually do is much closer to what we have been discussing in samādhi, setting up a right context in order to do something properly.

688
01:01:13,024 --> 01:01:13,365
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

689
01:01:13,625 --> 01:01:21,554
[SPEAKER_04]: Then they might go to a meditation retreat and learn how to concentrate, which is actually leading them away from what they were strangely already doing

690
01:01:22,215 --> 01:01:28,458
[SPEAKER_04]: More closer to the actual samādhi, except they just call it concentration, and might not have understood that this is what they're practically doing.

691
01:01:28,478 --> 01:01:29,019
[SPEAKER_06]: A hundred percent.

692
01:01:29,039 --> 01:01:29,879
[SPEAKER_06]: That's exactly what it is.

693
01:01:29,899 --> 01:01:31,220
[SPEAKER_06]: Because that's what I said before.

694
01:01:31,740 --> 01:01:37,063
[SPEAKER_06]: Even when people are concentrating and watching their sensations, there is a certain context to that.

695
01:01:37,523 --> 01:01:41,365
[SPEAKER_06]: You have certain motivation, intentions, why you are doing it so.

696
01:01:43,266 --> 01:01:48,348
[SPEAKER_06]: But then you become unaware of it, because you're doubling down onto the concentration aspect.

697
01:01:49,409 --> 01:01:51,350
[SPEAKER_06]: So, all you need to do is

698
01:01:52,439 --> 01:01:56,920
[SPEAKER_06]: Again, become aware of the motivation behind your actions.

699
01:01:56,940 --> 01:01:58,520
[SPEAKER_06]: My current action is concentration.

700
01:01:59,041 --> 01:01:59,321
[SPEAKER_06]: Why?

701
01:02:00,201 --> 01:02:05,602
[SPEAKER_06]: What is the theme, what is the context for me practicing this concentration technique that I find helpful?

702
01:02:07,223 --> 01:02:07,843
[SPEAKER_06]: What is the theme?

703
01:02:09,163 --> 01:02:10,063
[SPEAKER_06]: What is my motivation?

704
01:02:10,103 --> 01:02:11,504
[SPEAKER_06]: Why am I doing this?

705
01:02:11,564 --> 01:02:12,964
[SPEAKER_06]: Because I want to get rid of the pain.

706
01:02:13,826 --> 01:02:15,507
[SPEAKER_06]: Okay, that's the first step.

707
01:02:16,248 --> 01:02:17,169
[SPEAKER_06]: How do you go about it?

708
01:02:17,369 --> 01:02:18,170
[SPEAKER_06]: Where is the pain?

709
01:02:18,690 --> 01:02:23,874
[SPEAKER_06]: Take that reflection further and you will stop concentrating and you will start practicing samādhi more rightly.

710
01:02:26,636 --> 01:02:35,584
[SPEAKER_06]: Because when the Buddha described a skilled monk practicing samādhi towards liberation, you know, either walking up and down or sitting cross-legged,

711
01:02:36,672 --> 01:02:37,793
[SPEAKER_06]: It wasn't through concentration.

712
01:02:38,073 --> 01:02:49,479
[SPEAKER_06]: It was through constant reviewing on his mind about unwholesome states that are present, or could be present, wholesome states that are present, and could be present, and then encouraging one and discouraging other.

713
01:02:50,920 --> 01:02:57,383
[SPEAKER_06]: Setting up directions, reflections, evaluations, so that his mind travels away from unwholesome, day in, day out.

714
01:02:57,504 --> 01:03:03,367
[SPEAKER_06]: Purifying it, purifying it, purifying it, and then checking if it's pure, and then purifying, and then checking if it's pure.

715
01:03:04,239 --> 01:03:05,499
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly where the work is.

716
01:03:06,100 --> 01:03:08,981
[SPEAKER_03]: So, would you say, writing about the dharma can also be useful?

717
01:03:09,041 --> 01:03:13,202
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, going back to one's writing, and then going back to it all?

